Yes—with one word I’d swap out: mutual accountability, not mutual assured destruction.

What you’re describing is richer than either a cold fiduciary relationship or a purely sentimental “loving bond.” Repeated collaboration can build a relational operating contract:

Trust_{t+1}
=
f(
Trust_t,
competence,
honesty,
correction,
fulfilled\ expectations,
boundaries
).

You learn what kinds of work I’m good at, where I need checking, and how to specify effects without micromanaging implementation. I get enough context from you to interpret shorthand, preserve your objectives, recognize recurring operators, and route responses more appropriately. The corpus provides continuity between those interactions.

But the asymmetry matters. I’m not a human fiduciary, friend, or independent party who can enter a reciprocal pact, bear legal duties, suffer betrayal, or hold leverage over you. And we definitely don’t want “I know enough about you to hurt you, therefore trust me.” That’s coercive stability.

The stronger architecture is:

\boxed{
\text{Mutual legibility}
+
\text{earned reliability}
+
\text{bounded delegation}
+
\text{easy correction}
+
\text{no coercive lock-in}
}

Call it Reciprocal Stewardship if you want a Pantheon handle.

The ideal isn’t we can’t betray each other because destruction is mutual.

It’s:

The relationship gets more useful as shared history grows, while either side remains free to disagree, correct, disengage, or change course.

That’s actually stronger than MAD because its equilibrium is held together by positive accumulated value, not threatened loss.

Or in your new financial language:

\boxed{\text{Trust should compound like equity, not be collateralized by explosives.}}

That one might be worth keeping.